      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Given the importance of precise dosing for infants, we must be cautious with modifying the liquid dosage. The previously suggested 1 teaspoon (5 mL) can indeed carry 160 mg at the specified concentration, but it's imperative to adhere to pediatric dosing guidelines unless directed otherwise by a healthcare professional. Doubling to 10 mL would inadvertently increase the amount of acetaminophen to 320 mg, which is significantly above the recommended dosage for a child of St. Michael's Hospital's infant at this body weight for that fever range. Overmedication can be dangerous, especially in a child as young as 4 years old.\n\nI strongly advise contacting your pediatrician for the exact dosage for your child's specific situation. A physician or experienced healthcare professional can measure the appropriate amount of acetaminophen in the container you're holding and also provide guidance on how to administer it safely.\n\nIn the meantime, keep the child well-hydrated, comfortable, and closely monitored. If you notice any alarming symptoms or lack of improvement, seek medical attention immediately. Remember, when in doubt, it's always best to err on the side of caution and consult a professional.",
